export default {"id":"bassstation-level-6","number":6,"title":"Primary Oscillators (Osc 1 & Osc 2)","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"- Master the multiplexed front-panel control architecture, operating Oscillator 1 and Oscillator 2 independently using the shared control panel. - Configure and evaluate the four primary analogue waveforms: Sine, Triangle, Sawtooth, and Pul","modules":[{"id":"bassstation-m-6-1","code":"6","title":"Primary Oscillators (Osc 1 & Osc 2)","blurb":"- Master the multiplexed front-panel control architecture, operating Oscillator 1 and Oscillator 2 independently using the shared control panel. - Configure and evaluate the four primary analogue waveforms: Sine, Triangle, Sawtooth, and Pul","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"bassstation-l-o-6","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 6.1 — Primary Oscillators Architecture and Modulation","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Section Learning Outcomes","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Master the multiplexed front-panel control architecture, operating Oscillator 1 and Oscillator 2 independently using the shared control panel.","Configure and evaluate the four primary analogue waveforms: <strong>Sine</strong>, <strong>Triangle</strong>, <strong>Sawtooth</strong>, and <strong>Pulse/Square</strong>.","Calibrate octave registers using organ pipe-length designations (<strong>16'</strong>, <strong>8'</strong>, <strong>4'</strong>, <strong>2'</strong>) and understand concert pitch alignment.","Execute precise interval tuning and micro-tonal chorusing using the continuous <strong>Coarse</strong> (±1 octave) and <strong>Fine</strong> (±100 cents) controls.","Apply bipolar pitch modulation via <strong>Mod Env</strong> and <strong>LFO 1</strong>, utilizing exact musical interval depth landmarks.","Manipulate static <strong>Pulse Width</strong> (5% to 95% duty cycle) and dynamic <strong>Pulse Width Modulation (PWM)</strong> via LFO 2 and the Modulation Envelope."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-6-1-1","title":"6.1.1: The Multiplexed Shared-Control Surface Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Bass Station II implements a cost-effective, high-density physical control layout where a single set of rotary potentiometers, switches, and pushbuttons controls two completely independent discrete analog oscillator circuits (Osc 1 and Osc 2)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                      MULTIPLEXED OSCILLATOR CONTROL TOPOLOGY\n   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n   │ [Osc Select Switch] ───► [Microprocessor Parameter Router]             │\n   │                               │                                        │\n   │        ┌──────────────────────┴──────────────────────┐                 │\n   │        ▼                                             ▼                 │\n   │ [Digital Register: OSC 1]                   [Digital Register: OSC 2]  │\n   │  • Waveform: Sawtooth                        • Waveform: Sine          │\n   │  • Range: 8'                                 • Range: 4'               │\n   │  • Coarse: 0 / Fine: 0                       • Coarse: +7 / Fine: +8   │\n   │  • Mod Env: +12 / LFO 1: 0                   • Mod Env: 0 / LFO 1: +5  │\n   │  • Pulse Width: 50 (Manual)                  • Pulse Width: N/A        │\n   │        │                                             │                 │\n   │        ▼                                             ▼                 │\n   │ [Discrete Analogue DCO 1 Core]              [Discrete Analogue DCO 2 Core]\n   └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>State Independence</strong>:","Setting the <strong>Osc Select</strong> switch to <code>1</code> routes all physical knobs and switches to Oscillator 1's parameter registers.","Flipping the switch to <code>2</code> immediately disconnects the physical controls from Osc 1 and maps them to Osc 2.","<em>Memory Retention</em>: The parameters of the unselected oscillator remain locked in memory and continue driving the analog hardware.","<strong>Original Value Pickup LEDs (<code>◄</code> and <code>►</code>)</strong>:","When switching between Osc 1 and Osc 2, the physical knob positions will often not match the stored values of the newly selected oscillator.","Turning a knob activates the Original Value LEDs (<code>◄</code> or <code>►</code>), indicating the direction required to match the stored value, preventing unexpected parameter jumps."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load Init patch <code>64</code>. Ensure both Osc 1 and Osc 2 are turned up in the mixer.","Set <strong>Osc Select</strong> to <code>1</code>. Select <strong>Sawtooth</strong> and <strong>8'</strong> Range.","Set <strong>Osc Select</strong> to <code>2</code>. Select <strong>Square</strong> and <strong>4'</strong> Range.","Flip the switch back and forth between <code>1</code> and <code>2</code>. Observe that each oscillator maintains its distinct waveform and pitch octave without interference."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Does switching Osc Select from 1 to 2 alter the sound or settings of Oscillator 1?","[ ] How do the Original Value LEDs assist when editing an unselected oscillator?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-6-1-2","title":"6.1.2: Primary Waveform Topologies","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The primary oscillators feature four distinct wave-shaping networks driven by high-stability analog core circuits:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    PRIMARY OSCILLATOR WAVEFORM TOPOLOGY                    │\n├──────────┬──────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Waveform │ Panel LED    │ Harmonic Profile &amp; Circuit Character             │\n├──────────┼──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Sine     │ [Sin]        │ Pure fundamental. Zero harmonic distortion.      │\n│          │              │ Warm, subby, clean foundational low-end.         │\n├──────────┼──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Triangle │ [Tri]        │ Odd harmonics (\\(1, 3, 5, 7\\dots\\)) falling at  │\n│          │              │ \\(1/n^2\\). Smooth, woody, mellow acoustic color. │\n├──────────┼──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Sawtooth │ [Saw]        │ Complete integer series (\\(1, 2, 3, 4\\dots\\)) at │\n│          │              │ \\(1/n\\). Maximum harmonic density; rich &amp; bright.│\n├──────────┼──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Pulse /  │ [Pul]        │ Variable duty cycle. Odd harmonics when square;  │\n│ Square   │              │ shifted comb-filter notches as pulse thins.      │\n└──────────┴──────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Sine Wave</strong>:","Generates a single pure sinusoidal frequency. Ideal for deep sub-basses, clean organ foundations, or melodic leads requiring zero high-frequency harshness.","<strong>Triangle Wave</strong>:","Contains only odd harmonics, dropping off rapidly at a rate of 12 dB/octave. Provides more harmonic definition than a sine wave while remaining softer than a sawtooth.","<strong>Sawtooth Wave</strong>:","The primary subtractive baseline waveform. Contains every integer multiple of the fundamental, dropping off at 6 dB/octave. Essential for aggressive basslines, brass stabs, and wide detuned supersaw leads.","<strong>Pulse / Square Wave</strong>:","When set to 50% pulse width, produces a hollow, symmetrical square wave containing only odd harmonics. When pulse width is adjusted away from 50%, even harmonics are introduced, creating reedy, nasal, and biting tones."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In the mixer, turn <strong>Osc 1</strong> to 127 and all other sources to 0.","Open the <strong>Filter Frequency</strong> fully (127).","Press the <strong>Waveform</strong> button repeatedly to cycle through all four shapes.","Listen to the distinct timbral transition from pure sub-bass (Sine) to hollow woodwind (Triangle) to bright brass (Sawtooth) to nasal reed (Pulse)."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-6-1-3","title":"6.1.3: Pitch Octave Registers (Range Control)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Range</strong> rotary switch transposes the oscillator in precise octave steps using historical pipe-organ footage designations:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                  RANGE ROTARY SWITCH FOOTAGE MAP\n   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n   │ Footage │ Octave Offset │ Frequency Multiplier │ Register   │\n   ├─────────┼───────────────┼──────────────────────┼────────────┤\n   │ 16'     │ -1 Octave     │ \\(0.5 \\times f_0\\)   │ Sub-Bass   │\n   │ 8'      │ 0 (Concert)   │ \\(1.0 \\times f_0\\)   │ Standard   │\n   │ 4'      │ +1 Octave     │ \\(2.0 \\times f_0\\)   │ Tenor/Lead │\n   │ 2'      │ +2 Octaves    │ \\(4.0 \\times f_0\\)   │ High Lead  │\n   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>8' (Concert Pitch)</strong>: Standard reference octave. When keyboard Octave is set to <code>0</code>, striking the middle C key on the keybed produces Concert Middle C (C3, 261.63 Hz).","<strong>16' (Sub-Bass)</strong>: Drops oscillator pitch by 12 semitones.","<strong>4' (Tenor/Alto)</strong>: Raises oscillator pitch by 12 semitones.","<strong>2' (High Lead / Soprano)</strong>: Raises oscillator pitch by 24 semitones."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set Osc 1 to <strong>8'</strong> and Osc 2 to <strong>16'</strong>.","Balance both oscillators equally in the mixer.","Strike a single key; hear a powerful two-octave stacked octave bass patch."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-6-1-4","title":"6.1.4: Coarse and Fine Pitch Tuning","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Coarse</strong> and <strong>Fine</strong> rotary controls provide high-resolution pitch offset adjustments:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    COARSE AND FINE PITCH SPECIFICATIONS                    │\n├──────────┬──────────────────────┬──────────────────────┬───────────────────┤\n│ Control  │ Range                │ Display Readout      │ Musical Utility   │\n├──────────┼──────────────────────┼──────────────────────┼───────────────────┤\n│ Coarse   │ ±1 Octave            │ `-12` to `+12`       │ Fixed musical     │\n│          │ (±12 Semitones)      │ (0 = Center)         │ intervals (5ths)  │\n├──────────┼──────────────────────┼──────────────────────┼───────────────────┤\n│ Fine     │ ±1 Semitone          │ `-100` to `+100`     │ Acoustic chorusing│\n│          │ (±100 Cents)         │ (0 = Center)         │ &amp; micro-detuning  │\n└──────────┴──────────────────────┴──────────────────────┴───────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Coarse Tuning</strong>:","Continuous rotary potentiometer calibrated to transpose pitch up or down by up to 12 semitones (1 full octave).","<em>Musical Interval Landmarks</em>: Setting Coarse to <code>+7</code> creates a musical Perfect Fifth; <code>+5</code> creates a Perfect Fourth; <code>+4</code> creates a Major Third; <code>+3</code> creates a Minor Third.","<strong>Fine Tuning</strong>:","High-precision control providing ±100 cents (1 semitone) of fine pitch adjustment.","<em>Detuning Chorus Effect</em>: When Osc 1 and Osc 2 share the same waveform and octave, setting Osc 2 Fine tuning between <code>+5</code> and <code>+15</code> cents introduces acoustic phase beating, creating a rich, thick analog chorus without any digital effects."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On an Init patch, set both Osc 1 and Osc 2 to Sawtooth at 8' Range with equal mixer levels.","Slowly rotate <strong>Fine</strong> on Osc 2 from <code>0</code> to <code>+8</code>.","Listen to the phase cancellation and chorusing animate the sound.","Rotate <strong>Coarse</strong> on Osc 2 to <code>+7</code>. Play a single note; hear a powerful parallel Fifth interval chord."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] What is the semitone offset required to tune Oscillator 2 to a Perfect Fifth above Oscillator 1?","[ ] What is the tuning range of the Fine potentiometer in cents?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-6-1-5","title":"6.1.5: Pitch Modulation via Mod Env and LFO 1","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Oscillator section provides two dedicated bipolar rotary potentiometers for dynamic pitch modulation:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                  PITCH MODULATION DEPTH TOPOLOGY\n ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n │ [Mod Env Depth Pot] ──► Pitch Frequency Modulation via Envelope 2       │\n │   • Center (0) = Inactive                                                │\n │   • Clockwise (+1 to +127) = Positive Envelope (Pitch sweeps UP)         │\n │   • Counter-Clockwise (-1 to -127) = Inverted Envelope (Pitch sweeps DOWN)│\n ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n │ [LFO 1 Depth Pot] ──► Pitch Frequency Modulation via LFO 1 (Vibrato/FM)  │\n │   • Center (0) = Inactive                                                │\n │   • Value (0 to 127) = Depth of cyclic pitch oscillation                 │\n └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Mod Env (Envelope 2 to Pitch)</strong>:","Sweeps pitch according to the Attack and Decay stages of the Modulation Envelope.","Positive settings create rising attacks or classic laser/percussion pitch drops.","Negative settings invert the envelope curve, sweeping pitch up from below the fundamental.","<strong>Authoritative Depth Landmark Values (User Guide Calibration)</strong>:","For precise musical pitch sweeps via the Modulation Envelope, use the following calibrated display values:","Value <strong><code>6</code></strong> = <strong>1 Semitone</strong> (Minor 2nd sweep)","Value <strong><code>12</code></strong> = <strong>2 Semitones</strong> (Major 2nd sweep)","Value <strong><code>22</code></strong> = <strong>3 Semitones</strong> (Minor 3rd sweep)","Value <strong><code>32</code></strong> = <strong>5 Semitones</strong> (Perfect 4th sweep)","Value <strong><code>56</code></strong> = <strong>12 Semitones (1 Full Octave sweep)</strong>","Value <strong><code>80</code></strong> = <strong>24 Semitones (2 Full Octaves sweep)</strong>","<strong>LFO 1 to Pitch</strong>:","Introduces cyclic pitch vibrato (using Triangle wave) or trills (using Square wave).","When LFO 1 speed is raised into the audio range (~190 Hz), it introduces rich analog Frequency Modulation (FM) sidebands."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["On an Init patch, set Mod Env Attack to 0, Decay to 40%, Sustain to 0, Release to 0.","Set <strong>Mod Env</strong> pitch depth on Osc 1 to exactly <strong><code>56</code></strong>.","Strike a key; hear an exact one-octave pitch-drop transient ideal for electronic kick drums or synth brass attacks.","Set Mod Env depth back to 0. Turn <strong>LFO 1</strong> pitch depth to <code>15</code> with LFO 1 set to Triangle at moderate speed (~6 Hz); hear standard musical vibrato."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] What exact Mod Env depth value produces an exact 1-octave (12-semitone) pitch sweep?","[ ] What is the effect of turning the Mod Env depth potentiometer counter-clockwise into negative values?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-6-1-6","title":"6.1.6: Pulse Width and Pulse Width Modulation (PWM)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"6.1.6: Pulse Width and Pulse Width Modulation (PWM)","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"1. Technical Exposition","level":4},{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Pulse Width</strong> circuitry is operational exclusively when the <strong>Pulse</strong> waveform is active on the selected oscillator:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">               PULSE WIDTH MODULATION ARCHITECTURE\n ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n │ [PWM Source Switch]                                                     │\n │   ├── MANUAL  ──► [Pulse Width Pot] sets static duty cycle (~5% to 95%) │\n │   ├── MOD ENV ──► Envelope 2 dynamically modulates duty cycle over time │\n │   └── LFO 2   ──► LFO 2 continuously sweeps duty cycle back and forth   │\n └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Manual Pulse Width</strong>:","When <strong>PWM Source</strong> is set to <strong>Manual</strong>, the <strong>Pulse Width</strong> potentiometer directly sets the static duty cycle:","Center (Value <strong><code>50</code></strong>) = Perfect 50/50 Square wave (contains only odd harmonics; hollow, woody tone).","Values <strong><code>5</code> to <code>49</code></strong> or <strong><code>51</code> to <code>95</code></strong> = Narrow rectangular pulse (introduces even harmonics; sharp, nasal, reedy tone).","Values below ~5 or above ~95 narrow the pulse to a needle spike, attenuating fundamental energy.","<strong>Mod Env PWM</strong>:","When set to <strong>Mod Env</strong>, the Modulation Envelope dynamically alters pulse width over time, creating sweeping timbral shifts during note attack and decay.","<strong>LFO 2 PWM (Classic String Ensemble / Pad Texture)</strong>:","When set to <strong>LFO 2</strong>, LFO 2 continuously modulates the duty cycle.","Rotating the <strong>Pulse Width</strong> knob sets the <strong>modulation depth</strong> of LFO 2.","Produces the lush, sweeping, phase-chorused acoustic texture characteristic of vintage analog poly-synths and string machines."],"ordered":true},{"type":"heading","text":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","level":4},{"type":"list","items":["On Osc 1, select the <strong>Pulse</strong> waveform. Set PWM Source to <strong>Manual</strong>.","Turn the <strong>Pulse Width</strong> knob from 50 down to 10; hear the sound thin out into a reedy, nasal timbre.","Set PWM Source to <strong>LFO 2</strong>. Set LFO 2 to Triangle at a slow rate (~1 Hz).","Turn the <strong>Pulse Width</strong> knob to 75% depth; listen to rich, animated analog Pulse Width Modulation (PWM)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Before advancing to Module 7:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Explain how to edit Oscillator 2 without affecting Oscillator 1 using the shared control panel.","[ ] List the four primary oscillator waveforms and their harmonic characteristics.","[ ] What display value on the Mod Env pitch depth knob corresponds to an exact 2-octave pitch sweep?","[ ] Which LFO generator is hardwired to provide Pulse Width Modulation?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
